<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a zero-pressure accumulation conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A zero-pressure accumulation (ZPA) conveyor stops and queues products with controlled gaps between each item, eliminating product-to-product contact and backpressure. Each zone has independent drive control and a sensor. When a downstream zone is occupied, upstream zones hold their products with gaps. ZPA prevents product damage, label scuffing, carton crushing, and content shifting. <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors</a> are the most common ZPA technology.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">How does a ZPA conveyor work?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A ZPA (zero-pressure accumulation) conveyor divides the line into zones, each with its own drive and one photo-eye sensor. A zone stops only when it is occupied and the downstream zone is also occupied. Otherwise it runs. The discharge zone stops on an external signal from the downstream machine, and the queue builds back one zone at a time with a gap between products. Products can release one zone at a time (singulation) or all together (slug or train release). See <a href="#zpa-control-logic">How ZPA Control Logic Works</a>. <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR systems</a> use a low-voltage DC motorized roller per zone. <a href="/conveyor-types/live-roller-conveyors/">Belt-driven live roller</a> uses a pneumatic pressure frame and brake per zone, line-shaft uses pneumatic zone brakes, and chain-driven uses a gearmotor per zone.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What is the difference between ZPA and minimum-pressure accumulation?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Zero-pressure accumulation maintains gaps between every product with no contact. <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/minimum-pressure/">Minimum-pressure accumulation</a> allows light product-to-product contact, with drive pressure set so rollers slip under stopped product. Hytrol states its minimum-pressure conveyors can bring backpressure to 2% of the live load. ZPA costs more because every zone needs a sensor and drive control. ZPA is required for fragile or pressure-sensitive products. Minimum-pressure works for durable products like corrugated cases.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">How long should ZPA zones be?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Zone length must exceed the longest product in the direction of travel, plus the clearance the supplier's sensor placement needs. Zone lengths are set by the manufacturer: Hytrol's 190-NSPEZ manual lists 12, 18, 24, 30, and 36-inch zones. Size zones for the longest product in your mix. Advanced controllers can adapt to detected product length, allowing multiple short products per zone while maintaining ZPA behavior.</p>

</div>
</div>
